 be pushing  with my colleagues in the congress to  make that happen because i think  that as congress dithers on taking  meaningful steps americans are dying  every day coloradans are dying every day  before our eyes  and at some point we have to be prepared  to say enough is enough and i'm hopeful  that now is that time congressman we  don't yet know the details of this  particular case but the bigger picture  conversation about legislation doesn't  depend on any particular case  it depends on the politics of it i i  remember the new town  massacre and the feeling like if not now  when and yet those efforts largely came  to naught at least federally  what can you do now to make a difference  this time around  look we can't give up i mean americans  should be able to feel safe  in their grocery stores i i've gone to  this grocery store many times  they should be able to feel safe in  their schools in their movie theaters  and in their communities and so  yes as you said you know the the gun  lobby and so many others  have stopped the ability for us to make  meaningful reforms in the past but  that's no excuse  i think the american people are tired of  excuses so it's time for us to roll up  our sleeves  in the congress and muster the political  will power to actually get something  done  congressman have you heard any updates  from law enforcement uh there about what  happened  any more details you might have heard  either from there or from the governor's  office  uh we've been in touch of course with  the district attorney with the  chief of police and with the governor  i'll join the governor later this  morning for a briefing with the boulder  police and i suspect that we're all  going to learn a lot more  uh this morning when the boulder police  hold an additional media availability  and briefing i believe in  a couple hours here so obviously still  very much  you know an active crime scene there and  a lot of work to do in the days and  weeks ahead  both in terms of ensuring justice as the  district attorney so eloquently put it  but also  helping our community heal it's it's  gonna be rough  as we grieve with those families of  those who lost their loved ones  aren't you frustrated by this  congressman i look at countries around  the world that look at us and say what  is wrong with you united states of  america  i wake up this morning i keep thinking  about those 10 families who woke up and  the families in atlanta last week  whose lives are forever changed and we  keep pointing fingers at everybody else  and nothing gets done here  yeah no i i share your frustration gail  it doesn't as i said it doesn't have to  be this way this is not normal  in so many other western countries i  have a two and a half year old daughter  and i want her to grow up in a country  where she's not scared to go to the  grocery store he's not scared to go to  school  so that's certainly what motivates me  and  i i share your frustration i think a lot  of americans  share your frustrations right now we are  scared what is your message to the  boulder community today  my message is simple i i've again i know  this community i'm part of this  community and i would tell you gail that  the boulder community is strong  it is resilient it is kind and i know  that we will  uh you know ultimately come together to  support each other  and to support our community during this  very difficult time so  and our office will certainly be ready  and standing uh ready and willing and  able to help in that process  our condolences again to the boulder  community congressman joe nagooz thank  you so much for joining us this morning
 

